.TH CJSON_PARSE_MP 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cjson_parse_mp, cjson_parse \- parse a null-terminated JSON string into a DOM tree
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cjson.h>

cjson cjson_parse_mp(const char *json_str, char **err_str,
                     ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp);
/* convenience wrapper; default allocator */
static inline cjson cjson_parse(const char *json_str,
                                char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Parses json_str and returns the root node of the new DOM tree.
The parser accepts all seven kinds of JSON value:
  - null.
  - boolean.
  - integer, stored as long long.
  - number, stored as double.
  - string, stored as an owned heap copy in UTF-8.
  - array, stored as an ordered list of child nodes.
  - object, stored as string keys, each with a child node.

An object keeps its members in the order of the text; see
cjson_dictionary_first(3).  When a key appears more than once in one
object, the last value wins and the member keeps the place of the
first appearance.  The parser deep-frees every earlier value.  So
{"a":1,"b":2,"a":3} gives the members a = 3 and then b = 2.

The expected time of a parse stays linear in the number of members
of an object, even when the document comes from an untrusted peer.  The
object hashes its keys with a secret seed that the library chooses
once for each process, and an object whose keys collide anyway
switches to a hash keyed with a secret 128-bit key, so a peer cannot
keep a set of keys in one chain.  See chmap_create_full(3).

The parser stores an integer literal with no decimal point and no
exponent as CJSON_INTEGER.  It stores every other number as
CJSON_FLOAT.  This includes an integer that overflows long long.
A double holds an integer exactly only up to 2^53.  Therefore, an
integer literal above LLONG_MAX can lose its low digits after a parse
and a serialize.  Most JSON libraries do the same with large integers.
There is no limit on the length of the decimal text of a number
literal.  A number that overflows IEEE 754 double gives a parse
error.  Such a number is one for which strtod returns infinity.

String escape sequences \e", \e\e, \e/, \eb, \ef, \en, \er, \et, and
\euXXXX (with surrogate pairs) decode to UTF-8.  The parser rejects
the escape \eu0000 with a parse error.  cjson stores a string as a
null-terminated char * value, so a string cannot hold a null byte
inside it.

RFC 8259 section 8.1 needs JSON text to be UTF-8.  The parser
refuses a string or an object key that is not well-formed UTF-8,
with a parse error; it never repairs one.  Refused are a raw byte
sequence that is a truncated sequence, a continuation byte with no
lead byte, an overlong form, an encoded UTF-16 surrogate, a code
point above U+10FFFF, or a byte from 0xF5 to 0xFF, and a \euXXXX
escape of a surrogate that is not one half of a high-low pair, such
as \euD800 at the end of a string or \euDC00 alone.  The message
names the defect, the bytes as 0xNN or the escape, and the byte
offset in the input.  Every string that a parsed tree holds is
therefore well-formed UTF-8, and two keys that differ in their bytes
always stay two keys.

The parser rejects a document that nests more than 500 levels deep,
and gives a parse error for it.  Each array or object is one level,
in any combination, and a scalar adds none: 500 containers inside one
another, with any value inside the innermost, parse, and a 501st
container is refused.  The limit is a policy limit on which documents the
library accepts.  The parser walks the nesting with an explicit
worklist on the heap.  A document at the limit therefore costs the
same small, fixed amount of native stack as a flat one.  A parse is
safe on a thread with a small stack.  An ordinary JSON document
never comes near this depth.

The tree that a parse builds is linear in the length of the input.
An empty array or object costs one node.  The densest documents
(deep chains of one-member objects, or long arrays of one-member
objects) hold at most about 65 bytes of tree for each byte of input
on a 64-bit target.  A program that parses input from a peer it does
not trust bounds the memory of a parse by bounding the length of the
input.

cjson_parse() is a static inline wrapper that calls
cjson_parse_mp(json_str, err_str, NULL).
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
json_str  Null-terminated JSON text.
err_str   Out-parameter for the parse error message.  On success the
          function sets *err_str to NULL.  On failure it points
          *err_str at a null-terminated message that the LIBRARY
          owns.  Never free it.  It is not an allocation, and mp
          never sees it.  The text stays readable until the next
          FAILING parse on the same thread, which is the lifetime
          that strerror(3) gives.  The storage is per-thread, so two
          threads that parse at the same time never overwrite one
          another's message.  Copy the text to keep it longer.  Give
          NULL to throw the error message away.  The message is
          printable ASCII whatever the input holds: it quotes a
          printable ASCII byte of the input as it is, and spells
          any other byte as 0xNN.
mp        Custom memory management procedures for all nodes in the
          resulting tree, or NULL for the default allocator.
          The library keeps its own copy of the struct, so the caller
          may release or reuse the struct once this call returns. The
          functions that it names must stay callable until the last node
          built with them is destroyed. A process can use at most 64
          distinct sets of four functions; a call that would need a 65th
          fails and returns NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
The root cjson node on success.
NULL when the parse fails.  If err_str is not NULL, *err_str then
holds the reason.  That string belongs to the library, and the caller
never frees it.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The caller owns the tree that the function returns.  The caller must
free it with cjson_destroy().

The caller does NOT own the error string.  One rule covers the err and
err_str out-parameter of every module in this library: the library owns
the string and the caller never frees it.  See the note at the top of
common.h.

The parser always takes '.' as the decimal separator for a number,
as RFC 8259 asks.  The LC_NUMERIC locale of the calling thread does
not change this.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
char *err = NULL;
cjson doc = cjson_parse_mp(input, &err, NULL);
if (!doc) {
    fprintf(stderr, "parse failed: %s\en", err);
    return -1;
}
/* use doc ... */
cjson_destroy(doc);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_parse_n_mp (3),
.BR cjson_destroy (3),
.BR cjson_serialize (3),
.BR cjson_serialize_free_mp (3),
.BR cjson_dictionary_first (3)
